  "question": {
    "text": "If $2 a+3 b+6 c=0(a, b, c \\in R)$ then the quadratic equation $a x^2+b x+c=0$ has",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "G776q",
      "text": "\nat least one root in [0, 1]\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Eg0Vb",
      "text": "\nat least one root in [2, 3]\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "QdIrU",
      "text": "\nat least one root in [4, 5]\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CDdN3",
      "text": "\nnone of these",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{a x^3}{3}+\\frac{b x^2}{2}+c x \\Rightarrow f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=\\frac{a}{3}+\\frac{b}{2}+c=\\frac{2 a+3 b+6 c}{6}=0$\nAlso $f(x)$ is continuous and differentiable in $[0,1]$ and $\\left[0,1]\\right.$. So by Rolle's theorem, $f^{\\prime}(x)=0$. i.e. $a x^2+b x+c=0$ has at least one root in $[0,1]$",
    "image": null
  },
